From a2e01ee58c53abdf73c3f1043f39fd43b32bb80d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael Bommarito <michael.bommarito@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 21 Apr 2026 11:34:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] kernel: mark kthread() and smpboot_thread_fn() notrace
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Both functions end in do_exit() rather than a normal return. The
generic function_graph tracer pushes a shadow-stack entry at
their entry expecting a matching pop on return; that pop never
fires, leaving a stale entry on current->ret_stack.

On native x86 the leaked entry is harmless — the ret_stack walk
doesn't dereference stale entries because frame unwinding never
takes that path. On User-Mode Linux (UML) the walker DOES
dereference the leaked retp: UML resumes tasks via
kernel_longjmp into host-thread contexts whose stack addresses
don't match the pushed retp, so when a graphed function on the
unwind path returns, return_to_handler pops a stale entry and
jumps through its corrupt return slot. Observed crash: SEGV in
schedule+0x4a with RAX=0 and return_to_handler on the stack
twice — the stale pointer got dereferenced and followed.

The narrow fix is to skip graph tracing at these two specific
generic-kernel entry points. User-written threadfns that
kthread() calls are still graph-traceable; only the wrappers
themselves are excluded. Matches the analysis in
Documentation/virt/uml/redesign/04-risks/decisions-log.md D34
§Alternatives considered (1), now in scope under D45's in-fork
scope policy.

No functional impact on native architectures — notrace here
only suppresses push/pop at these two call sites, which already
don't need the graph event on any architecture (the functions
exist to be the landing pad for a threadfn, not to be
themselves the subject of trace analysis).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 kernel/kthread.c | 22 +++++++++++++++++++++-
 kernel/smpboot.c | 11 ++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 31 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/kernel/kthread.c b/kernel/kthread.c
index 791210daf8b4..0243c371dbe3 100644
--- a/kernel/kthread.c
+++ b/kernel/kthread.c
@@ -377,7 +377,27 @@ static void kthread_affine_node(void)
 	free_cpumask_var(affinity);
 }

-static int kthread(void *_create)
+/*
+ * notrace: the generic function_graph tracer pushes a shadow-
+ * stack entry at kthread() entry, expecting a matching pop on
+ * return. kthread() does not return — it ends in do_exit().
+ * On native x86 the leaked entry is harmless (the ret_stack
+ * walk tolerates stale entries because frame unwinding doesn't
+ * dereference the leaked retp). On UML the walker DOES
+ * dereference, because UML resumes tasks via kernel_longjmp
+ * into a different host-thread context whose stack addresses
+ * don't match the leaked retp — the stale pointer then gets
+ * popped via a graphed function's ret and jumped through.
+ * Observed crash: SEGV in schedule+0x4a (RAX=0) with
+ * return_to_handler on the stack twice. See UML redesign
+ * decisions-log D34 for the full three-leak-source analysis.
+ *
+ * The narrow fix (matching the D34 2026-04-21 addendum under
+ * D45) is to skip graph tracing at this specific entry. Any
+ * user-written kthread threadfn is still graph-traced; only
+ * the generic kthread()-the-wrapper is skipped.
+ */
+static notrace int kthread(void *_create)
 {
 	static const struct sched_param param = { .sched_priority = 0 };
 	/* Copy data: it's on kthread's stack */
diff --git a/kernel/smpboot.c b/kernel/smpboot.c
index 4503b60ce9bd..1b85a27103c8 100644
--- a/kernel/smpboot.c
+++ b/kernel/smpboot.c
@@ -99,7 +99,16 @@ enum {
  *
  * Returns 1 when the thread should exit, 0 otherwise.
  */
-static int smpboot_thread_fn(void *data)
+/*
+ * notrace: reached via kthread_create_on_cpu() and entered from
+ * the generic kthread() wrapper; ends in do_exit() rather than a
+ * return. See kernel/kthread.c::kthread for the full rationale
+ * on why the unterminated graph-shadow-stack push is observable
+ * as a crash specifically on UML (kernel_longjmp-based task
+ * resume walks a ret_stack whose leaked retp doesn't match the
+ * actual stack). Same fix pattern; UML redesign D34 addendum.
+ */
+static notrace int smpboot_thread_fn(void *data)
 {
 	struct smpboot_thread_data *td = data;
 	struct smp_hotplug_thread *ht = td->ht;
--
2.53.0

